-- The 2023 Merger Guidelines, section 3: rebuttal evidence showing that no substantial lessening of competition is
-- threatened by the merger. AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW: each result says whether the Agencies accept or credit
-- the rebuttal as the Guidelines describe it ("The Agencies apply those tests consistent with prevailing law", section 3,
-- [pdf page 31 of 51]); a court applies the law, not these results.
--
-- An answer is NOTHING when an input is impossible (a negative price, value or time) or when the answer turns on a case
-- the text does not decide: an alternative offer at or below liquidation value whose classification changes whether the
-- defense is accepted or the claim credited (fork FF-2).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mg-nouns`

§ `3. Rebuttal Evidence Showing that No Substantial Lessening of Competition is Threatened by the Merger`

§§ `3.1 Failing Firms`

-- Footnote 62 ([pdf pages 31 and 32 of 51]): "Any offer to purchase the assets of the failing firm for a price above the
-- liquidation value of those assets will be regarded as a reasonable alternative offer." and "Liquidation value is the
-- highest value the assets could command outside the market." The liquidation value is the caller's figure. The text does
-- not say that an offer at or below liquidation value is unreasonable, so for such an offer the answer is NOTHING (fork
-- FF-2). A negative price or liquidation value is impossible, and the text says nothing of it: NOTHING.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, footnote 62 [pdf pages 31 and 32 of 51]
GIVEN offer IS AN `Alternative offer`
      `liquidation value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the offer is a reasonable alternative offer, by footnote 62` MEANS
    IF     `liquidation value` LESS THAN 0
        OR offer's `the price offered for the assets` LESS THAN 0
        OR NOT (offer's `the price offered for the assets` GREATER THAN `liquidation value`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST TRUE

GIVEN offer IS AN `Alternative offer`
      `liquidation value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offer is above liquidation value` IF
    offer's `the price offered for the assets` GREATER THAN `liquidation value`

-- Requirement A ([pdf page 31 of 51]): "the grave probability of a business failure". "The Agencies typically look for
-- evidence ... that the allegedly failing firm would be unable to meet its financial obligations in the near future.
-- Declining sales and/or net losses, standing alone, are insufficient to show this requirement."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, requirement A [pdf page 31 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Failing firm claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `requirement A of the failing firm defense is shown` IF
        claim's `the firm would be unable to meet its financial obligations in the near future`
    AND NOT (claim's `the evidence of failure is declining sales or net losses standing alone`)

-- Requirement B ([pdf page 31 of 51]): "The prospects of reorganization of [the failing firm are] dim or nonexistent";
-- the Agencies look for evidence that the firm "would be unable to reorganize successfully under Chapter 11".
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, requirement B [pdf page 31 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Failing firm claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `requirement B of the failing firm defense is shown` IF
    claim's `the firm would be unable to reorganize successfully under Chapter 11`

-- Requirement C ([pdf page 31 of 51]): the acquirer "is the only available purchaser"; the Agencies look for "unsuccessful
-- good-faith efforts to elicit reasonable alternative offers that pose a less severe danger to competition than does the
-- proposed merger". Footnote 62: "Parties must solicit reasonable alternative offers before claiming that the business is
-- failing." Read with the offers above liquidation value (the only ones footnote 62 calls reasonable).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, requirement C, footnote 62 [pdf pages 31 and 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Failing firm claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `requirement C of the failing firm defense is shown` IF
        claim's `good-faith efforts were made to elicit reasonable alternative offers, before the claim`
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN o YIELD     `the offer is above liquidation value` o (claim's `the liquidation value of the assets`)
                                AND o's `the offer poses a less severe danger to competition than the proposed merger`)
                 (claim's `the alternative offers received`))

-- Footnote 62 ([pdf page 32 of 51]): "If a reasonable alternative offer was rejected, the parties cannot claim that the
-- business is failing." Read as written, for any rejected offer above liquidation value (fork FF-1).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, footnote 62 [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN offers IS A LIST OF `Alternative offer`
      `liquidation value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a reasonable alternative offer was rejected` IF
    any (GIVEN o YIELD `the offer is above liquidation value` o `liquidation value` AND o's `the offer was rejected`) offers

-- Fork FF-2. Footnote 62 calls an offer above liquidation value reasonable and says nothing of one at or below it. Each
-- reading below is computed in full: on reading (a) such an offer is not reasonable, so only offers above liquidation
-- value count; on reading (b) it is reasonable, so every offer counts. Counting more offers can only defeat requirement C
-- (or condition 2) or add a bar, never help the claim, so when (a) and (b) agree, every way of classifying the offers at
-- or below liquidation value agrees too, and the answer is the text's.
GIVEN offer IS AN `Alternative offer`
      `liquidation value` IS A NUMBER
      `on reading (b) of fork FF-2` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offer counts as a reasonable alternative offer, on a reading of fork FF-2` IF
       `the offer is above liquidation value` offer `liquidation value`
    OR `on reading (b) of fork FF-2`

GIVEN offers IS A LIST OF `Alternative offer`
      `liquidation value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an offer at or below liquidation value was received` IF
    any (GIVEN o YIELD NOT (`the offer is above liquidation value` o `liquidation value`)) offers

GIVEN claim IS A `Failing firm claim`
      `on reading (b) of fork FF-2` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the failing firm defense is accepted, on a reading of fork FF-2` IF
        `requirement A of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
    AND `requirement B of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
    AND claim's `good-faith efforts were made to elicit reasonable alternative offers, before the claim`
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN o YIELD     `the offer counts as a reasonable alternative offer, on a reading of fork FF-2` o liq `on reading (b) of fork FF-2`
                                AND o's `the offer poses a less severe danger to competition than the proposed merger`)
                 offers)
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN o YIELD     `the offer counts as a reasonable alternative offer, on a reading of fork FF-2` o liq `on reading (b) of fork FF-2`
                                AND o's `the offer was rejected`)
                 offers)
    WHERE
        liq MEANS claim's `the liquidation value of the assets`
        offers MEANS claim's `the alternative offers received`

GIVEN offers IS A LIST OF `Alternative offer`
      `liquidation value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a price or value is impossible` IF
       `liquidation value` LESS THAN 0
    OR any (GIVEN o YIELD o's `the price offered for the assets` LESS THAN 0) offers

-- 3.1 ([pdf pages 31 and 32 of 51]): "the failing firm defense has three requirements"; the Supreme Court has "confine[d]
-- the failing company doctrine to its present narrow scope", so a weak position that does not meet them is no defense.
-- The requirement and bar fields count the offers footnote 62 calls reasonable (reading (a) of fork FF-2); the last field
-- says when an offer at or below liquidation value was also received, in which case only the accept field is the answer
-- on both readings.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1 [pdf pages 31 and 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Failing firm claim`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Failing firm finding`
`the Agencies' reading of the failing firm claim` MEANS
    IF     `a price or value is impossible` (claim's `the alternative offers received`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the assets`)
        OR NOT (`the failing firm defense is accepted, on a reading of fork FF-2` claim FALSE EQUALS `the failing firm defense is accepted, on a reading of fork FF-2` claim TRUE)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`Failing firm finding` WITH
        `requirement A: a grave probability of business failure` IS `requirement A of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
        `requirement B: prospects of reorganization dim or nonexistent` IS `requirement B of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
        `requirement C: the acquirer is the only available purchaser` IS `requirement C of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
        `a reasonable alternative offer was rejected, so the business cannot be claimed failing` IS `a reasonable alternative offer was rejected` (claim's `the alternative offers received`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the assets`)
        `the Agencies accept the failing firm defense` IS
                `requirement A of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
            AND `requirement B of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
            AND `requirement C of the failing firm defense is shown` claim
            AND NOT (`a reasonable alternative offer was rejected` (claim's `the alternative offers received`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the assets`))
        `an offer at or below liquidation value was received, and the defense is accepted or not on both readings of it` IS `an offer at or below liquidation value was received` (claim's `the alternative offers received`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the assets`))

§§ `3.1 footnote 64: a failing division`

-- Footnote 64 ([pdf page 32 of 51]): "The Agencies do not normally credit claims that the assets of a division would exit
-- the relevant market in the near future unless: (1) applying cost allocation rules that reflect true economic costs,
-- the division has a persistently negative cash flow on an operating basis, and such negative cash flow is not
-- economically justified for the firm by benefits such as added sales in complementary markets or enhanced customer
-- goodwill; and (2) the owner of the failing division has made unsuccessful good-faith efforts to elicit reasonable
-- alternative offers that would keep its assets in the relevant market and pose a less severe danger to competition than
-- does the proposed acquisition." And "the Agencies require evidence that is not solely based on management plans that
-- could have been prepared for the purpose". Footnote 62's reading of a reasonable offer is applied to a division, and
-- its bar on a rejected one only to an offer that would keep the assets in the relevant market, the only offers footnote
-- 64 asks for (fork FD-1).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, footnote 64 [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Failing division claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `condition 1 of footnote 64 is shown` IF
        claim's `on cost allocation reflecting true economic costs, the division has a persistently negative operating cash flow`
    AND NOT (claim's `the negative cash flow is economically justified for the firm by benefits such as complementary sales or goodwill`)
    AND NOT (claim's `the evidence rests solely on management plans that could have been prepared for the purpose`)

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, footnote 64 [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Failing division claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `condition 2 of footnote 64 is shown` IF
        claim's `the owner made good-faith efforts to elicit reasonable alternative offers`
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN o YIELD     `the offer is above liquidation value` o (claim's `the liquidation value of the division's assets`)
                                AND o's `the offer would keep the assets in the relevant market`
                                AND o's `the offer poses a less severe danger to competition than the proposed merger`)
                 (claim's `the alternative offers for the division`))

-- Fork FD-1: a rejected offer that would take the assets out of the market is no alternative to their exit, and footnote
-- 64 counts only offers "that would keep its assets in the relevant market", so only such an offer bars the claim.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, footnotes 62 and 64 [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN offers IS A LIST OF `Alternative offer`
      `liquidation value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a reasonable alternative offer keeping the assets in the market was rejected` IF
    any (GIVEN o YIELD     `the offer is above liquidation value` o `liquidation value`
                       AND o's `the offer would keep the assets in the relevant market`
                       AND o's `the offer was rejected`)
        offers

GIVEN claim IS A `Failing division claim`
      `on reading (b) of fork FF-2` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the failing division claim is credited, on a reading of fork FF-2` IF
        `condition 1 of footnote 64 is shown` claim
    AND claim's `the owner made good-faith efforts to elicit reasonable alternative offers`
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN o YIELD     `the offer counts as a reasonable alternative offer, on a reading of fork FF-2` o liq `on reading (b) of fork FF-2`
                                AND o's `the offer would keep the assets in the relevant market`
                                AND o's `the offer poses a less severe danger to competition than the proposed merger`)
                 offers)
    AND NOT (any (GIVEN o YIELD     `the offer counts as a reasonable alternative offer, on a reading of fork FF-2` o liq `on reading (b) of fork FF-2`
                                AND o's `the offer would keep the assets in the relevant market`
                                AND o's `the offer was rejected`)
                 offers)
    WHERE
        liq MEANS claim's `the liquidation value of the division's assets`
        offers MEANS claim's `the alternative offers for the division`

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.1, footnote 64 [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Failing division claim`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Failing division finding`
`the Agencies' reading of the failing division claim` MEANS
    IF     `a price or value is impossible` (claim's `the alternative offers for the division`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the division's assets`)
        OR NOT (`the failing division claim is credited, on a reading of fork FF-2` claim FALSE EQUALS `the failing division claim is credited, on a reading of fork FF-2` claim TRUE)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`Failing division finding` WITH
        `condition 1: a persistently negative cash flow, not economically justified` IS `condition 1 of footnote 64 is shown` claim
        `condition 2: no reasonable alternative offer keeping the assets in the market and less dangerous` IS `condition 2 of footnote 64 is shown` claim
        `a reasonable alternative offer keeping the assets in the market was rejected` IS `a reasonable alternative offer keeping the assets in the market was rejected` (claim's `the alternative offers for the division`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the division's assets`)
        `the Agencies may credit the claim that the division's assets would exit` IS
                `condition 1 of footnote 64 is shown` claim
            AND `condition 2 of footnote 64 is shown` claim
            AND NOT (`a reasonable alternative offer keeping the assets in the market was rejected` (claim's `the alternative offers for the division`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the division's assets`))
        `an offer at or below liquidation value was received, and the claim is credited or not on both readings of it` IS `an offer at or below liquidation value was received` (claim's `the alternative offers for the division`) (claim's `the liquidation value of the division's assets`))

§§ `3.2 Entry and Repositioning`

-- [pdf page 32 of 51]: entry induced by the merger must be "timely, likely, and sufficient in its magnitude, character,
-- and scope to deter or counteract the competitive effects of concern."
-- "Timeliness. ... entry must be rapid enough to replace lost competition before any effect from the loss of competition
-- due to the merger may occur. ... Moreover, the entry must be durable". The two times are in any one unit (fork EN-2).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.2, Timeliness [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Entry claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the entry is timely` IF
        claim's `time until the entry would replace the lost competition` LESS THAN claim's `time until any effect from the loss of competition may occur`
    AND claim's `the entry would be durable`

-- "Likelihood. Entry induced by lost competition must be so likely that no substantial lessening of competition is
-- threatened by the merger." The caller's finding.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.2, Likelihood [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Entry claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the entry is likely` IF
    claim's `the entry is so likely that no substantial lessening of competition is threatened`

-- "Sufficiency. ... Entry must at least replicate the scale, strength, and durability of one of the merging parties to be
-- considered sufficient. The Agencies typically do not credit entry that depends on lessening competition in other
-- markets." ([pdf page 33 of 51]). One merging party matched on all three (fork EN-1).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.2, Sufficiency [pdf page 33 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Entry claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the entry is sufficient` IF
        any (GIVEN c YIELD     c's `the entry replicates its scale`
                           AND c's `the entry replicates its strength`
                           AND c's `the entry replicates its durability`)
            (claim's `comparisons with the merging parties`)
    AND NOT (claim's `the entry depends on lessening competition in other markets`)

-- Footnote 65 ([pdf page 32 of 51]): "Repositioning is a supply-side response that is evaluated like entry. ...
-- Repositioning that would reduce competition in the markets from which products or services are moved is not a
-- cognizable rebuttal for a lessening of competition in the relevant market."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.2, footnote 65 [pdf page 32 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Entry claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supply response is a cognizable rebuttal` IF
    CONSIDER claim's `the response claimed`
    WHEN `entry` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `repositioning` THEN NOT (claim's `the repositioning would reduce competition in the markets the products are moved from`)

@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.2 [pdf pages 32 and 33 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Entry claim`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Entry finding`
`the Agencies' reading of the entry claim` MEANS
    IF     claim's `time until the entry would replace the lost competition` LESS THAN 0
        OR claim's `time until any effect from the loss of competition may occur` LESS THAN 0
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`Entry finding` WITH
        `timely` IS `the entry is timely` claim
        `likely` IS `the entry is likely` claim
        `sufficient` IS `the entry is sufficient` claim
        `a cognizable rebuttal` IS `the supply response is a cognizable rebuttal` claim
        `the Agencies credit the entry or repositioning rebuttal` IS
                `the entry is timely` claim
            AND `the entry is likely` claim
            AND `the entry is sufficient` claim
            AND `the supply response is a cognizable rebuttal` claim)

§§ `3.3 Procompetitive Efficiencies`

-- "Merger Specificity. The merger will produce substantial competitive benefits that could not be achieved without the
-- merger under review. Alternative ways of achieving the claimed benefits are considered" ([pdf page 33 of 51]).
-- Footnote 70: "The Agencies will credit the merger specificity of efficiencies only in the presence of evidence that a
-- contract to achieve the asserted efficiencies would not be practical."
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.3, Merger Specificity, footnote 70 [pdf page 33 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Efficiencies claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the efficiencies are merger-specific` IF
        claim's `the merger would produce substantial competitive benefits`
    AND null (claim's `alternatives that could achieve the benefits`)
    AND claim's `there is evidence that a contract to achieve the efficiencies would not be practical`

-- "Verifiability. These benefits are verifiable, and have been verified, using reliable methodology and evidence not
-- dependent on the subjective predictions of the merging parties or their agents." ([pdf page 34 of 51])
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.3, Verifiability [pdf page 34 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Efficiencies claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the efficiencies are verified` IF
    claim's `the benefits have been verified by reliable methodology not dependent on the parties' subjective predictions`

-- "Prevents a Reduction in Competition. To the extent efficiencies merely benefit the merging firms, they are not
-- cognizable. The merging parties must demonstrate through credible evidence that, within a short period of time, the
-- benefits will prevent the risk of a substantial lessening of competition in the relevant market." ([pdf page 34 of 51])
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.3, Prevents a Reduction in Competition [pdf page 34 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Efficiencies claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the efficiencies prevent a reduction in competition` IF
        claim's `credible evidence shows the benefits will, within a short period, prevent the risk of a substantial lessening of competition in the relevant market`
    AND NOT (claim's `the efficiencies merely benefit the merging firms`)

-- "Not Anticompetitive. Any benefits claimed by the merging parties are cognizable only if they do not result from the
-- anticompetitive worsening of terms for the merged firm's trading partners." Footnote 71: "The Agencies will not credit
-- efficiencies if they reflect or require a decrease in competition in a separate market." ([pdf page 34 of 51])
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.3, Not Anticompetitive, footnote 71 [pdf page 34 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Efficiencies claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the efficiencies are not anticompetitive` IF
        NOT (claim's `the benefits result from an anticompetitive worsening of terms for trading partners`)
    AND NOT (claim's `the benefits reflect or require a decrease in competition in a separate market`)

-- "Procompetitive efficiencies that satisfy each of these criteria are called cognizable efficiencies." Then: "To
-- successfully rebut ..., cognizable efficiencies must be of a nature, magnitude, and likelihood that no substantial
-- lessening of competition is threatened by the merger in any relevant market. Cognizable efficiencies that would not
-- prevent the creation of a monopoly cannot justify a merger that may tend to create a monopoly." And at the start of
-- 3.3: "the Agencies will not credit vague or speculative claims, nor will they credit benefits outside the relevant
-- market" ([pdf pages 33 and 34 of 51]; and Guideline 10, [pdf page 28 of 51]).
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 3.3 [pdf pages 33 and 34 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS AN `Efficiencies claim`
GIVETH AN `Efficiencies finding`
`the Agencies' reading of the efficiencies claim` MEANS `Efficiencies finding` WITH
    `merger specificity` IS `the efficiencies are merger-specific` claim
    `verifiability` IS `the efficiencies are verified` claim
    `prevents a reduction in competition` IS `the efficiencies prevent a reduction in competition` claim
    `not anticompetitive` IS `the efficiencies are not anticompetitive` claim
    `the efficiencies are cognizable` IS `the efficiencies are cognizable` claim
    `the Agencies credit the efficiencies as rebutting the evidence of harm` IS
            `the efficiencies are cognizable` claim
        AND NOT (claim's `the claim is vague or speculative`)
        AND claim's `the benefits are in the relevant market where competition may be lessened`
        AND claim's `the efficiencies are of a nature, magnitude and likelihood that no substantial lessening of competition is threatened in any relevant market`
        AND ((NOT (claim's `the merger may tend to create a monopoly`)) OR claim's `the efficiencies would prevent the creation of a monopoly`)
    WHERE
        GIVEN c IS AN `Efficiencies claim`
        GIVETH A BOOLEAN
        DECIDE `the efficiencies are cognizable` IF
                `the efficiencies are merger-specific` c
            AND `the efficiencies are verified` c
            AND `the efficiencies prevent a reduction in competition` c
            AND `the efficiencies are not anticompetitive` c

§§ `2.5.A.2 footnote 31: the elimination of double marginalization`

-- Footnote 31 ([pdf page 17 of 51]): "The Agencies examine whether elimination of double marginalization satisfies the
-- approach to evaluating procompetitive efficiencies in Section 3.3, including examining: (a) ...; (b) whether contracts
-- short of a merger have eliminated or could eliminate double marginalization such that it would not be
-- merger-specific, and (c) ...". (b) decides merger specificity; (a) and (c) are examinations with no stated outcome,
-- reported together.
@ref Merger Guidelines 2023, section 2.5.A.2, footnote 31 [pdf page 17 of 51]
GIVEN claim IS A `Double marginalization claim`
GIVETH A `Double marginalization finding`
`the Agencies' reading of the double marginalization claim` MEANS `Double marginalization finding` WITH
    `the double marginalization claim is not merger-specific` IS claim's `(b) contracts short of a merger have eliminated or could eliminate double marginalization`
    `the claim is examined further under the four criteria of Section 3.3` IS NOT (claim's `(b) contracts short of a merger have eliminated or could eliminate double marginalization`)
    `examinations (a) and (c) both favour the claim` IS
            claim's `(a) the merged firm will be more vertically integrated as a result of the merger`
        AND claim's `(c) the merged firm has the incentive to reduce price, given the effect on its sales of the related product to rivals`
